  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE...Prolonged spell of dry weather is expected through \nthe workweek, to go along with a warming trend to temperatures. \n\nSouthern New England remains entrenched in a cool cyclonic flow \npattern, leading to mostly sunny to partly cloudy skies, modest \nnorthwest breezes and near to slightly cooler than seasonable \ntemperatures with chilly nighttime temperatures. Expect more of the \nsame through Monday night, although with a bit less breeziness \nMonday. Still enough of a NW gradient tonight to offer lows around \nthe freezing mark (close to daytime dewpoints), but light/calm winds \nMonday night could point to Monday night being rather cold with \nforecast lows in the mid 20s outlying valleys to lower to mid 30s. \n\nFor Tuesday and really into most of the workweek, strong high \npressure at surface and rising 500 mb heights aloft govern Southern \nNew England, with modest westerly winds. Expect clear skies and a \nlengthy stretch of dry weather ahead, one with strong diurnal \ntemperature ranges - increasingly mild days but cool nights. \nTemperatures could trend quite a bit above normal as we move into \nThursday and Friday, as 925 mb temps rise to around +10 to +12C, and \nit wouldn't shock if we see temps reach into the mid to perhaps even \nupper 60s. Our next best chance for precipitation may not \nmaterialize until the weekend as the ridge tries to break down, \nbut even that is far from set in stone.",
